WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.500
Good morning, John.

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.820
Today I want to delve into a peculiar practice of that most mysterious of cultures,

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:06.140
the American.

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:08.080
It's a deeply illogical practice,

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:09.300
economically troubling,

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.160
psychologically dubious-

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:14.400
I will regularly give money to people who are not asking for it.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.500
I'm not required by law to do this

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.320
but I feel as if I should.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:19.720
And I don't know why I feel this way;

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:23.800
I've recently discovered that I don't know why I feel a lot of the ways that I feel.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.060
I'm kind of an insane person.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:28.949
For example, I spend many of my afternoons watching videos about goat sex and fictional soccer teams.

00:00:28.949 --> 00:00:29.720
But anyway,

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:33.540
to start off our discussion about tipping, I want to go into my personal rules

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:34.480
which are,

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:35.700
as it happens,

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.420
the best rules for how to tip.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:39.580
If you are in a restaurant that has a JAR,

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:41.219
a dollar goes in the jar,

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:42.440
if I have a dollar.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:43.760
If I don't have a single,

00:00:43.769 --> 00:00:45.260
then you're screwed.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:49.020
You sit down and someone comes to you to bring you food and talk to you

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:51.600
then that person deserves, at the very minimum,

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:52.740
fifteen percent.

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:55.180
But almost never do they get 15%,

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:58.100
because there are three ways that they can increase their tip.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:00:58.600
ONE.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:00:59.729
Good service.

00:00:59.729 --> 00:01:00.640
TWO.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:03.400
If I feel particularly nice that day.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:04.460
THREE.

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:06.180
The math is difficult

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:07.980
and so I round up.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:09.460
My personal inability to do math in my head

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:11.920
is probably responsible for my most generous tips.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:13.280
If at the end of step two,

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:16.049
I have not gotten above two dollars per person,

00:01:16.049 --> 00:01:18.220
I give two dollars per person,

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:19.860
Leaving a 25% tip

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:21.480
on a three dollar bill

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.020
is not good tipping.

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:24.400
Now, next thing,

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.700
after a deep psychological investigation of my own inner self,

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:29.180
I have figured out

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:32.240
that what I would like to be the reason for me giving tips

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:35.040
is that those people are making me happy so I want to make them happy.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:35.540
But,

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:38.000
there are a lot of lesser motives that I believe are, in fact,

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.620
much more responsible.

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:42.540
Waitstaff make less than 50% of their living off of their wages,

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:45.580
most of their living is from tips and so I would feel bad.

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:47.020
Also, I live in a very small town

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:50.000
and the person who's waiting on me is probably friends with someone who I'm friends with

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.350
and I don't want it getting around that I'm a jerk face.

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:55.480
And I think the number one reason why I do it

00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:57.320
is because it's the thing to do.

00:01:57.329 --> 00:01:59.939
The more I think about it, the more I realize that pretty much everything I do,

00:01:59.939 --> 00:02:01.860
I do because it's the thing to do

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:04.559
and I don't know why the thing to do is the thing to do,

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:05.600
it just is.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.860
Sometimes, I feel like my entire life is just one long string of

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:09.260
"That's the thing to do."

00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:10.720
Get out "thing to do"!

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:11.960
I don't need you!

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:13.520
I make my own things to-

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:14.200
I don't!

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:15.200
My brain

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.180
is encapsulated by this culture,

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:19.980
and it makes me do all the "things to do".

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:20.940
Not that I mind,

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:23.060
I mean, most of "things to do" are pretty cool.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.320
Now, the reason why tipping is supposed to be the thing to do,

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:26.550
is because we want to encourage

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:27.700
good service.

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:29.000
It is important to note

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:30.140
that this does not work.

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:33.820
Things that influence tip more than the quality of service include:

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:35.540
the ethnicity of the waiter or waitress,

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:37.720
the gender of the waiter or waitress,

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:40.620
the weather outside on that particular day,

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:42.120
the cost of the meal

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:45.540
and whether or not they've drawn a smiley face on the check.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:46.100
Smiley face,

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:47.360
interestingly,

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:49.640
raises the tip of waitresses by 9%,

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:52.280
lowers the tip of waiters by 3%.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:53.720
W T F.

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:54.560
Here's the thing about tipping,

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:56.960
96% of Americans do it every single time,

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:59.520
but in lots of other countries, that's just not a thing that could st-

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:00.799
In fact, in like Japan and China

00:03:00.799 --> 00:03:02.180
it's considered an insult.

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:04.540
In Europe, where there are service charges included in the check,

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:05.520
higher wages

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:06.340
and also

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:07.880
like medical care

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:09.160
that's inexpensive or free,

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:12.563
waiters and waitresses don't need tips in order to be above the poverty line.

00:03:12.563 --> 00:03:13.640
In the United States, however,

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:15.600
we have this idea that everything should be

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:16.900
controlled by the market,

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:18.920
which is how the UK ends up with television shows like

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:20.140
QI and Doctor Who

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:22.500
and we are stuck with documentaries about

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:23.711
fictional apocalypses

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:25.040
on the History Channel.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:25.920
And that

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:28.140
is why tipping kills Time Lords.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:30.380
I don't- where- how did I get here?

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:32.329
Leave the most interesting comment to win

00:03:32.329 --> 00:03:33.800
my winnie-the-pooh-sters.

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:34.880
John, I will see you on Monday,

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:36.260
when you celebrate

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:37.820
our FIFTH anniversary.

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:39.580
It's a wonderful life.

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:41.380
Don't forget to be awesome and if you're in America,

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:43.880
don't forget to tip at least $2 per seat.

